Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time, the disciples came to Jesus and asked him, "Who is the most important in the Kingdom of Heaven?"
Jesus called a little child to Him, and set the child in the midst of them.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do that, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
Whoever becomes the greatest, like this child, is the most important in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Whoever welcomes a child like this in my name, I will welcome them.
But what if someone established just one of these little ones who believe in me over the sin? If he did that, it would be better for him to be hanged with a big grinder around his neck and drowned at the bottom of the sea.
How will it be evil for the world because of the things that lead people to sin! It must come things like this. But how will it be evil for those who do these things!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or one foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to sin, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hellfire. The story of the lost sheep.
Look that you despise not one of these little ones. For I say unto you: Their angels in heaven always behold the face of my Father which is in heaven.
<.>
What do you think? Suppose a man has 100 sheep, and one of them wanders away. Doesn't he leave the 99 sheep on the hills? Doesn't he go after the one that wandered off?
What I am going to tell you is the truth. If he finds that sheep, he is happier with the one than with the ninety-nine that did not go astray.
He is also with your father in heaven. No one wants any of these little ones to go astray. When someone sins against you.
If your brother wrongs you, go to him. Tell him what the wrong action was. Keep the matter between the two of you. If he listens to you, you have restored him to you.
But what if he does not listen to you? Then take two or three men with you. The Scriptures say: "No fact can be established except on the testimony of two or three witnesses." (Deut. 19:15)
But what if he also refuses to hear the witnesses? Then report it to the church. And what if he also refuses to hear the church? Let him be to you as a non-believer or as a tax collector.
What I am about to tell you is truth. What you bind on earth will be bound in heaven. What you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.
Here's what I say to you again. Suppose the two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for. My Father who is in heaven will do it for you.
"Where two or three are gathered in my name, there I am with them." "The servant who had not compassion."
Peter approached Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The kingdom of heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants regarding what they had collected for him.
When the king started doing this, a man who owed millions of dollars was brought to him.
The man could not pay. So he issued his master order. The man, his wife, his children, and everything he could sell to pay his debt.
The server fell to his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will return everything."
His master felt sorry for him. He forgave him what was due and allowed him to go.
But then that servant went out and found another servant who owed him some dollars. So he grabbed him and began to strangle him. He said, "Pay what you owe!"
The other servant fell on his knees. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will come back to you."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and threw the man into prison. The man would stay there until he could repay what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. This greatly disturbed them. They went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called the first servant. He said, "You wicked servant, I forgave you all that debt because you desired me to do so.
Shouldn't you have mercy on the other servant just as I have shown mercy to you?
Furious, his master handed him over to the guards. He would be punished until he repaid everything he owed.
This is how your Father in heaven will treat each of you if you don't forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
Large crowds followed him. He healed them there.
Some Pharisees came to test him, and they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus' answer, "Haven't you read that the one who created them at the beginning 'made them male and female' (Genesis 1:27)?"
He said, "This is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
There is no one but one body, as long as God has joined what no human can separate.
They asked, "So why did Moses command that a man write a divorce document for his wife and send her away?"
Jesus answered, "Moses allowed you to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard. But it was not like that from the beginning."
Thus I tell you, whoever divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery. It is not permissible for a man to divorce his wife unless she is unfaithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If that is how it is between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone is able to accept the idea of remaining single. Only those who have been helped to live without marrying can accept that."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. Some were made that way by other people. And others made themselves that way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living that way must do so." And children were brought to Jesus.
Some people brought their little children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to go away.
Jesus said, "Let the little children come to me and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he left there. Jesus and the rich young man
A man came to Jesus. He asked, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to receive eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, speak the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness."
Honor your father and your mother. (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) And you shall love your neighbor as yourself. (Leviticus 19:18)
The young man said, "All these commands have been obeyed. What must I do next?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell what you have and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When this young man heard that, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "What I am about to tell you is true. It is hard for rich people to enter the kingdom of heaven."
Again I tell you, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for someone who is rich to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were really amazed. They asked, "Then who can be saved?" When the disciples heard this, they were greatly astonished and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "With people this is impossible, but with God all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! So what will be given to us as rewards?"
Truly, truly, I tell you, said Jesus, "When everything is made new, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. And you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones. You will be judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
Everyone who has left their home or family or fields for my sake will receive a hundred times more. And they will also inherit eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the vineyard workers.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who was a landowner. He went out early in the morning to hire workers to work in his vineyard.
She agreed to give them the usual wage for a day's work. Then He sent them to His vineyard.
After nine o'clock that morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market square doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard. I will give you what is right."
They left. "He went out again around noon and at three o'clock, and did the same thing."
Around five o'clock, he went out and found others standing around. He asked them, "Why have you spent the whole day standing here doing nothing?"
They said, "Because nobody has employed us." He said to them, "You too, go and work in my vineyard."
When evening came, the owner of the vineyard spoke to the person who was in charge of the workers. He said, 'Call the workers and give them their pay. Begin with the last ones I hired. Then go on to the first ones.' Translation: عندما جاء المساء، تكلم صاحب الكرم مع الذي كان مسؤولاً عن العمال. فقال: "ادع العمال وأعطهم أجورهم. ابدأ بالأخيرين الذين استأجرتهم، ثم انتقل إلى الأولين."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock came. Each of them received the usual daily wage.
So when the time comes for those who were hired first to expect that they will be entitled to more, but each one of them has earned the normal daily wage for one day.
When they received it, they began to suspect its owner.
They said, "These are the people who were last hired, and they only worked for one hour. You paid them the same as you paid us. We are the ones who did most of the work and stayed out in the scorching sun all day."
The owner answered one of them. He said to him, "My friend, I am treating you with justice. Have we not agreed that you will work for the wage of a normal working day?"
Take your money and leave. I want to give those I hired last the same wage I gave you.
Isn't it my right to do what I want with my money? Do you feel cheated because I gladly gave to others?
Those who were last will be first, and those who were first will be last. Jesus repeats speaking about his death.
Jesus is heading to Jerusalem. On the way, He took the twelve disciples aside to speak to them.
He said, "We are going up to Jerusalem. The Son of Man will be handed over to the chief priests and the teachers of the law. They will condemn him to death."
Then they will hand him over to the power of non-Jews, and they will mock him and flog him, and they will rob him, and they will crucify him. And on the third day he will rise from the dead!" Umm Neama asks Jesus
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus. Her two sons came with her. And she kneeling down asked him a favour.
What do you want? Jesus asked. "Promise me that one of my two sons will sit at your right in your kingdom," she said. "Promise me that the other will sit at your left."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them: "You will drink for my sake a cup. And yet it is not for me to say who sits at my right hand or at my left. For these places are for those whom my Father has prepared."
The ten remaining gentlemen heard about this. They were angry at the two brothers.
Jesus called his disciples and said to them, "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great ones exercise authority over them.
Do not be like this. Rather, whoever wants to be great among you, let him be your servant.
And whoever wants to be first must be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve. And to chew up his life as a ransom for many. Two out of the blind had their sight restored.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. Great crowds were following him.
Two blind men sitting by the road heard that Jesus was passing by. They cried out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to be quiet. They told them to shut up. But the two men cried out all the more, "Have mercy on us, O Lord, O Son of David!"
Jesus stood up and called out to them. "What do you want me to do for you?" he asked.
"Lord," they answered, "we wish to see."
Jesus suffered deep concern for them. He touched their eyes. And just by that they could see. And they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And when they all approached Jerusalem, they came to Bethphage. It was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two disciples.
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. When you reach it, you will find a donkey tied up, with its young one with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If someone tells you something, tell them that the Lord needs them. The owner of the place will send them immediately.
This happened so that what was spoken through the prophet might be fulfilled. It says,
Say to the city of Zion's daughter, "Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and having salvation is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a colt the foal of a donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a colt. They put their clothes on them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
A very large crowd spread their clothes on the road. Others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road.
Some people went ahead of him, and some people followed him. They all cried out: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus. He is the Prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus cleared the Temple.
Jesus entered the mosque of the House. He began to drive out all those who were selling and buying there. He overturned the tables of those who were exchanging currency. He also overturned the chairs of those who were selling pigeons.
He said to them: "It is written: 'My house shall be called a house of prayer.'"
The blind and the lame came to Jesus at the temple. There he healed them.
The chief priests and the teachers of the law saw the wonderful things he did. They also saw the children in the temple area shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" When they saw this, they were indignant.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. "Yes," Jesus answered. "Have you not read in the Scriptures: 'The very young will praise you, both the young men and the women, will proclaim your praise.' (Psalm 8:2)"
Then Jesus went out from among the people and went out of the city to Bethany. He spent the night there. The fig tree withered.
Early in the morning, Jesus was on his way back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree by the road. He came up to it, but found nothing on it but leaves. Then he said to it, "May you never again bear fruit!" And immediately it dried up.
When the disciples saw it, they were amazed. "How did the fig tree wither so quickly?" they asked.
Jesus answered, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must have faith and not doubt. Then you can do what was done to the fig tree. And you can say to this mountain, 'Go and throw yourself into the sea.' And it will be done."
"If you believe, you will receive what you ask for when you pray." authority of Jesus is questioned
Jesus entered the temple courts. As he taught there, the chief priests and the teachers of the law came to him. "By what authority are you teaching here?" they asked. "Who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question. If you answer me, I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
From where did John's baptism come? From heaven or from men?" They discussed the matter with each other. They said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say, "Who are the men"? We fear people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "Jesus, we do not know." Jesus said, "Nor will I tell you by what authority I do these things." The Parable of the Two Sons.
What do you think about this? There was a man who had two sons. He went to the first son and said, "My son, go today and work in the vineyard."
"No, I will," the son answered. But then he changed his mind and went.
Then the father went to the other son. He told him the same thing. And the son replied, "I will go, sir." But he did not go.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" "The first," they answered. Jesus said to them, "The truth is, I tell you now, that tax collectors and prostitutes enter the Kingdom of God ahead of you."
John came to show you the true way to live, and you did not believe in him. But all the tax collectors and sinners believed in him. You saw this. But even then, you did not repent and believe in him. The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. There was a man who owned some land. He planted a vineyard. He put a wall around it. He dug a pit to press the grapes in. He also built a watchtower. He rented the vineyard to farmers. Then he went on a trip.
When the harvest time approached, he sent his servants to the tenants. He instructed the servants to collect the fruit quota that belonged to him.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. They killed another. They threw the third slave down with stones to kill him.
Then the man sent other slaves to the tenants. He sent more than he had sent at first. The tenants treated these slaves in the same way.
Finally, he sent his son to them. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the Son approaching. They said to one another, 'This is the one who will receive all the owner's possessions someday. Come, let's kill him. Then everything will be ours.'
And they seized him and cast him outside the vineyard, and then they killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
"They will destroy those wicked people," they answered. "Then he will rent the vineyard to other renters. They will give him his share of the harvest at harvest time."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was written in the Scriptures: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone. This is from the Lord and is wonderful in our eyes'? (Psalm 118: 22, 23)."
So if it is like this, I say to you: The kingdom of God will be taken from you and given to a people who will bear its fruits.
Whosoever falls on this stone will be broken to pieces. But on whomever the stone falls, it will grind him to powder.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard Jesus' parables. They realized he was speaking about them.
So they sought to arrest him, but they feared the crowd. For the people regarded him as a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
He taught them other signs. He said to them:
Here is an example of the Kingdom of Heaven. The king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those who had been invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come, but they refused.
Then he sent other servants. He said, "Tell those who were invited: I have prepared my dinner; I have killed my oxen and my fatlings. All is ready. Come to the marriage feast."
But the people did not care. One of them went to his field. Another went to his work.
The others seized his servants. They treated them badly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to destroy them. They killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to his servants, "The wedding dinner is ready. But those I invited are not worthy to come."
Go to street corners. Invite anyone you find to dinner.
The slaves went out into the streets. They gathered all the people they found, whether they were good or bad. Soon, the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king went in to see the guests. He noticed a man who was not dressed in wedding clothes.
"Friend," he said to him, "how did you get in here without a wedding garment?" The man could not say anything.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet, then throw him outside into the darkness. There people will weep and grind their teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out. They judged that they should hunt down the Christ with his own words.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. They said, "Teacher, we know that you are a man of integrity. You teach the way of God in truth. You do not let others dictate to you what to say or do. You do not care about how important others are."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right to pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus knew their evil intentions. He said, "You hypocrites! Why are you trying to trap me?"
"Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes." So they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them: "Who is in this picture? And who are these words?"
They said, "Render unto Caesar the things that are Caesar's." Then He said to them, "Render unto Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and unto God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished. So they left him and went away. Marriage, when the dead rise.
That same day, the Sadducees came to Jesus with a question. They do not believe that people rise from the dead. That day, جاء الصدوقيون إلى يسوع بسؤال. إنهم لا يعتقدون أن الناس يقومون من الأموات.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses told us. If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry the widow. He must have children to carry on his brother's name."
There were seven brothers among us. The first of them got married and died. He had no children, so he left his wife for his brother.
The same thing happened with the second, third, and fourth brothers. He continued until he reached the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
Then, when the dead arise, which one of them is his wife? All seven of them were married to her.
Jesus came with the answer: "You are mistaken, because you do not know the Scriptures, nor the power of God."
When the dead rise, they will not marry, nor will their parents give them in marriage. They will be like the angels in heaven.
What about the resurrection of the dead? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. I am the God of Jacob." (Exodus 3:6) He is not the God of the dead. He is the God of the living.
When the crowds heard this, they were astonished at his teaching. The most important commandment is...
The Pharisees heard that the Sadducees were unable to answer Jesus. So the Pharisees gathered together.
One of them, an expert in the law of Moses, tested Jesus with a question.
"Teacher," he asked, "which commandment in the Law is the most important?"
Jesus answered, " 'Love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul and with all your mind.' " (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And a similar one: "Love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets is based on these two commandments. From whom is Christ descended?
The disciples were gathered together. Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Who is his son? "The son of David," they answered.
He said to them, "Why then does David call him 'Lord'? For the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said:
The Lord said to my Lord, "Sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David calls him "Lord," how can he be David's son?
No one has been able to answer it with a single word. Since that day, no one has dared to ask him any questions.
Jesus condemns the Pharisees and teachers of the law.
Jesus showed compassion to the crowds and to his disciples.
He said, "The scribes and Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
You do not obey them. Do everything they tell you to do. But do not do what they do. They do not do what they preach.
They load heavy burdens on others' shoulders, but they themselves don't want to lift a finger to move them.
All that they do they do to be seen of men. On their foreheads and arms they wear small boxes containing texts from the Holy Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the epaulettes on their coats very long.
They like to sit in places of honor at dinners. They also like to have the most important places in synagogues.
They like to be greeted in the markets. They like to be called "rabbi".
But you should not be called "Rabbi". You have only one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father; for you have one Father, who is in heaven.
He should not be called "Master". You have one Master, the Christ.
The most important among you is your servant.
He who exalts himself will be humiliated, and he who is humiliated will be exalted.
O ye wretched teachers of the Namos and the Far فرنسيسians! Ye are hypocrites! Ye shut the Kingdom of Heaven against men. Ye neither go in yourselves, nor suffer ye them that are entering to go in.
<.>
Woe to you, you teachers of the law! You hypocrites! Snakes! You brood of vipers! How can you escape being condemned to hell?
Woe to you, you blind guides! You say: If anyone swears by the Temple, it is nothing; but if anyone swears by the gold of the Temple, he is bound by his oath.
You are blind and foolish! What is more important? Is it the gold? Or is it the temple which makes the gold sacred?
You also said, "If anyone takes an oath by the altar, it is nothing; but if anyone takes an oath by the gift that is on it, he is bound to keep his oath."
O most blind of all people! What is more important? Is it the guidance? Or is it the altar upon which the guidance is made sacred?
Whoever swears by the sacrificial animal is swearing by it and by everything on it.
And anyone who swears by the temple swears by it and by him who dwells in it.
And whoever swears by the heaven, then indeed he swears by the throne of Allah and by whoever sits on it.
Woe to you, O learned and very clever scholars, who are really only hypocrites! You render to Caesar the things which are Caesar's, like the tithes of herbs like parsley and mint and cumin. But you neglect the weightier matters of the law: justice, mercy, and faith. These ought to have been done, and not left undone.
O blind guide! You strain out the gnat of small insects from your food, but you swallow the camel whole!
Woe to you, you teachers of the law! You hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of decay and death on the inside. You are like wells that are clean on the outside but are full of muddy water and corruption on the inside. You strain out a gnat but swallow a camel.
You blind Pharisee! First clean the inside of the cup and the dishes, then the outside will also be clean.
Woe to you, teachers of the law! You hypocrites! You are like whitewashed tombs, which look beautiful on the outside but are full of the bones of the dead and everything unclean.
It is the same with you. From the outside it looks like you are doing what is right. But on the inside you are full of what is wrong. You pretend to be what you are not.
Woe to you, O scholars and Pharisees, you hypocrites! Indeed, you are building the tombs of the prophets and decorating the tombs of the righteous.
And you say: If it were not for us, we would have been in the days of those who passed before us. We would not have done what they were doing, and we would not have had authority over the prophets.
You bear witness against yourselves. You acknowledge that you are the sons of those who killed the prophets.
Complete the sins that those before you started!
O nest of flames! How will you escape your hellish reign?
I sent to you prophets, wise men, teachers, and you killed some of them, you crucified some of them, you flogged some of them in your assemblies, and you expelled them from village to village.
And you will pay for the blood of all the good people that has been shed on earth. I mean from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah the son of Berekiah. Zechariah is the one you killed between the temple and the altar.
What I will tell you is true. All of this will happen to those who are living now.
Jerusalem! Jerusalem! You who kill the prophets and stone those sent to you. Many things I have wanted to gather your people together. I wanted to be like a hen that gathers her brood under her wings. But you were not willing!
Look, your house is empty.
I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord." (Psalm 118:26)
The Symptoms of the Resurrection
Jesus went out of the temple. As he was walking, his disciples approached him. They wanted to direct his attention to the buildings of the temple.
"Do you see all these things?" Jesus asked. "Truly I tell you, not one stone will be left on another; every stone will be thrown down."
Jesus was sitting on the Mount of Olives. There, His disciples came to Him privately. They said to Him, "Tell us, when will these things happen? And what is the sign of Your coming? And what is the sign of the end of the age?"
Jesus' answer, "Be on your guard! Beware that no one deceives you."
Many will come in my name, saying, "I am the Messiah!" and they will deceive many.
You will hear of wars. And you will also hear people speaking of future wars. Do not be afraid. Those things must happen. But the end has not come yet.
Nations will fight nations. Kingdoms will fight kingdoms. People will go hungry. There will be earthquakes in many places.
All these are the beginning of the birth pangs.
Then the people will hand you over to be tortured and killed. All nations will hate you because of me.
In those days, many will depart from their faith. Some will hate one another. Some will betray one another to his enemies.
Many false prophets will appear. They will deceive many people.
Because evil is increasing, the love of most people will grow cold.
But whoever stands firm to the end will be saved.
This good news about the kingdom will be preached in the whole world. It will be a testimony to all nations. Then the end will come.
The prophet Daniel spoke of "the detestable thing that causes desolation." (Daniel 9:27; 11:31; 12:11) One day you will see it standing in the holy place. Let the reader understand this.
Then those who are in Judea, let them flee to the mountains.
No one on the roof should go down to their house to take out anything.
No one in the field should have to come back to get their coat.
I will be a great affliction in those days for the pregnant! I will be a great affliction for the nursing mothers!
Pray that you do not have to flee in winter or on a Saturday.
There will be great distress in those days; it will be worse than at any time since the beginning of the world. And it will never be like that again.
If the time were not cut short, no one would survive. But because of the elect, it will be cut short.
At that time, someone might say to you, "Look! Here is the Christ!" or "There he is!" Do not believe it.
The Antichrist and false prophets will appear. They will perform great and marvelous miracles. They will try to deceive God's chosen people if possible.
If indeed, I have already told you beforehand.
If someone tells you, "He is far in the desert," do not go out to him. Or if someone says, "He is deeply inside the house," do not believe him.
The lightning that comes from the east will be seen in the west. So it will be when the Son of Man comes.
Vultures will gather wherever there is a carcass.
After those pain-filled days, "the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light; the stars will fall from the sky, and the planets will be destroyed." (Isaiah 13:10; 34:4)
Then the sign of the Son of Man will appear in heaven, and all the nations of the earth will mourn. They will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven with great power and glory.
He will send His angels with the sound of a great trumpet. They will gather His chosen ones from the four corners of the earth. They will come with them from the farthest reaches of the heavens to its farthest depths.
Learn a lesson from the fig tree. Once its branches are smooth and its leaves sprout, you know that summer is near.
Likewise, when you see all these things happening, you know that the end is near. It is at the door.
What I am now going to tell you is the truth. Those living at that time will not die until all those things take place.
The heavens and the earth shall perish, but my words shall not perish. The day and the hour are unknown.
No one knows about that day or that hour, except the Father alone. Not even the angels in heaven know about it, nor the Son, but only the Father.
Remember how it was in the days of Noah. It will be the same when the Son of Man comes.
In the days before the flood, people were eating and drinking. They were marrying. They were giving their daughters in marriage. They were doing all those things until the day Noah entered the ark.
They did not know what was going to happen until the flood came and took them all. So it will be when the Son of Man comes.
There will be two men in the field. One will be taken, and the other will be left.
Two women will grind with a hand mill, and one will be taken and the other will be left behind.
Therefore, prepare yourselves. You do not know on which day your Lord will come.
You must understand something. Suppose the homeowner knew at what time of night the thief would come. Then he would have remained vigilant. He would not have allowed the thief to enter his home.
And you too must be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour when you do not expect.
Imagine a master who appoints one of his servants to oversee the other servants in his house. The servant's duty is to provide them with their food at the appropriate time. The master seeks a faithful and wise servant for this task.
It will be better for the servant if the master finds him working when the master returns.
What I am going to tell you now is the truth. The master will make that servant responsible for everything he owns.
But suppose that this slave is wicked; suppose that he says to himself, "My master stays away so long."
Assume he starts hitting the other servants. And assume he eats and drinks with those who drink testicles.
The master of that servant will come to him one day when the servant does not expect his master. He will come at an hour of which the servant knows nothing.
Then the master will cut the lady into pieces. He will send her to the place where the protesters go. There the people will weep and grind their teeth.
The Story of the Ten Virgins
Here is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like at that time. Ten virgins took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.
Five of them were foolish. Five were wise.
The fools took their lamps, but they didn't take any olive oil with them.
The wise ones took oil in jars with their lamps.
The suitor did not come for a long time. So all the maidens of honour grew tired and fell asleep.
In the middle of the night, someone shouted: "Here's the groom! Come out to meet him!"
Then all the virgins of the bridal prepared their lamps.
The foolish said to the wise, "Give us of your oil. For our lamps are going out."
"No," they answered. "There may not be enough for everyone. Instead, go to those who are selling the oil. Buy some for yourselves."
So they went to buy oil. But while they were on their way, the bridegroom arrived. And the virgins who were ready with him went in to the wedding feast. Then the door was shut.
After that, the rest of my lunar bridal party came. "Oh, my lord! My lord!" they cried. "Open the door for us!"
But he answered, "What I am going to tell you is true. I do not know you."
Keep watch, then. You do not know the day or the hour when the bridegroom will come. The Story of the Three Servants
Again, this is what the Kingdom of Heaven will be like. There was a man going on a journey. He called his servants and gave them authority over his possessions.
He gave ten thousand dollars to one. And he gave four thousand dollars to another. And he gave two thousand dollars to the third. The man gave each servant the amount of money that he knew the servant could embrace. Then he went on his journey.
The server who received the ten thousand dollars immediately went and put his money to work. He earned another ten thousand dollars.
The one who got four thousand dollars got four thousand dollars more.
But the man who received two thousand dollars went and dug a hole in the ground. He hid his master's money there.
After a long time, the master of those servants returned. He wanted to gather all the money they had earned.
The man who received ten thousand dollars came with the other ten thousand dollars. He said, "Sir, you have trusted me with ten thousand dollars. Look, I have earned another ten thousand dollars."
His master said to him, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share in my joy!"
The man who had four thousand dollars also came. He said, "Sir, you have brought me four thousand dollars. Look, I have earned another four thousand dollars."
His master said to him, "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful in a few things, so I will put you in charge of many things. Come and share in my joy!"
Then the man to whom two thousand dollars had been paid came. He said, "Master, I knew that you were a hard man. You sow where you have not sown. You gather where you have not scattered.
I was afraid, so I left and paid you two thousand dollars on the ground. Look, here is what belongs to you.
His master said to him, "You wicked, lazy slave! Did you know that I gather where I did not sow, and I gather where I did not scatter?
Then you must put my money in the bank. When I return, to take it back with interest.
Then his master ordered the other servants saying: "Take the two thousand dollars from him who has it. Give it to him who has twenty thousand dollars."
To those who have, more will be given, and they will have more than enough. But from those who have nothing, even what little they have will be taken away.
Cast out that worthless servant. There in the darkness, people will weep and grind their teeth. "The sheep and the goats."
The Son of Man will come in his glory. And he will come with all the angels. Then he will sit on his throne in the glory of the heavens.
All nations will be gathered before him. He will separate people into two groups. It will be like a shepherd separating the sheep from the goats.
He will make the sheep to his right and the he-goats to his left.
Then the King will speak to those on His right. He will say, "Blessed by My Father are you. Come, inherit the Kingdom prepared for you since the creation of the world."
I was hungry. So you brought me food. I was thirsty. So you brought me a drink. I was a stranger. So you invited me into your home.
When I needed clothes, you gave them to me. When I was sick, you took care of me. When I was in prison, you visited me.
Then the righteous will answer: "Lord, when did we see you hungry and feed you? When did we see you thirsty and give you a drink?
When did we see you as a stranger and invite you in? When did we see you in need of clothes and give them to you?
"When did we see you sick or in prison and come to you?"
The King will answer: "What I tell you is true. Whatever you did for one of the least of these brothers of mine, you did it for me."
Then He will say to those on His left, 'Cursed ones! Go away from Me to the everlasting fire prepared for the devil and his angels.'
I was hungry. But you did not give me anything to eat. I was thirsty. But you did not give me anything to drink.
I was a stranger, but you did not take me into your homes. I needed clothes, but you did not give me anything. I was sick and in prison, but you did not look after me.
They will also say, 'Lord, when did we see you hungry or thirsty and did not help you? When did we see you as a stranger or needing clothes or sick or in prison and did not help you?'
He will say, "What I tell you is true. Whatever you did not do for one of the least of these, you did not do for me."
Then they will go to be tormented forever. But those who did good will obtain eternal life.
The plan to kill Jesus.
When Jesus completes saying all these words, He said to the disciples...
As you know, Easter is in two days. The Son of Man will be delivered to be crucified.
Then the chief priests and the elders of the people assembled. They gathered in the palace of Caiaphas, the high priest.
They made plans to arrest Jesus cunningly. They wanted to kill him.
"But not during the feast," they said. "The people might riot." A woman poured perfume on Jesus.
Jesus was in the house of Lazarus. He was in the house of a man named Simon, who had a skin disease.
A woman came to Jesus with his sealed jar of very expensive perfume. She poured the perfume on his head as he sat at the table.
When the students saw that, they were angry. "Why this waste?" they asked.
Goodness might be sold at a high price. Money might be given to poor people.
Jesus knew about it, so he said to them, "Why do you trouble this woman? For she has done a good work for me."
You will always have the poor with you. But you will not always have me.
I perfumed my body to prepare for my burial.
What I am about to tell you is the truth. What you have done will be told everywhere the good news is preached throughout the world. It will be told as a memorial of her." Judas agrees to hand Jesus over
And one of the twelve went to the chief priests, named Judas Iscariot.
He said, "What will you give me if I hand Jesus over to you?" So they counted out for him thirty pieces of silver.
From that time, Judas was waiting for an opportune time to deliver Jesus to them. The Lord's Supper
It was the first day of the Feast of Unleavened Bread. The disciples came to Jesus, saying, "Where do you want us to prepare for you to eat the Passover?"
He said, "Go into the city to a certain man. Say to him, 'The Teacher says: "My time is near. I will keep the Passover at your house with my disciples." ' "
So the disciples did what Jesus had instructed them. They prepared the Passover meal.
When evening came, Jesus was at the table with the Twelve.
And as they were eating, he said to them, "Truly I say to you, one of you will betray me to my enemies."
The disciples were deeply grieved. Each one of them began to say to him, "Is it I, Lord?"
Jesus answered, "The one who dips his hand with me in the bowl is the one who will betray me."
The son of man will go as it is written about him. But how will it be? Evil for the one who delivers up the son of man! It would have been better for him if he had not been born.
Juda was the one who was going to betray him. He said, "Don't I, Lord?" Jesus answered, "Yes. You are the one."
And as they were eating, Jesus took bread. He thanked and broke it. He gave it to his disciples and said, "Take and eat. This is my body."
Then he took the cup. He thanked God and gave it to them. He said, "You all drink from it."
This is my blood for a new covenant. It is poured out for the forgiveness of the sins of many.
This is what I tell you. From now on, I will never drink wine with you again until the day I drink it with you in my Father's kingdom.
Then they sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus says that Peter will fail.
Jesus said to them: "This night all of you will disown me because of me. For it is written: 'I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered.' (Zechariah 13:7)"
But after I am raised from the dead, I will go ahead of you to Galilee."
Peter replied, "All the others might turn away because of you. But I never will."
"What I am about to tell you is true," Jesus answered. "It will happen tonight. Before the rooster crows, you will deny me three times."
But Peter said, "May I never fall into disgrace! But I will not boast about my courage, for fear that I might fall into disgrace." And all the disciples said the same thing. Jesus Prays in Gethsemane.
Then Jesus went with his disciples to a place called Gethsemane. He said to them, "Sit here while I go over there and pray."
Peter took with him James and John, the sons of Zebedee. And he began to be sorrowful and very heavy.
Then he said to them, "My soul is very sorrowful. I feel that death is near. Stay here. Watch over me."
He went a little further. Then he fell on his face to the ground. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from me. But not as I will, but as you will."
Then he came back to his disciples and found them sleeping. He said to Peter, "Could you not watch with me for one hour?"
Behold, and pray. Then ye shall not fall into temptation, when the trial is given. The spirit is willing. But the flesh is weak.
Jesus went away again. He prayed, saying, "My Father, if it is possible, may this cup be taken from me. Yet not what I will, but what you will."
Then he came back. He found them asleep again. They couldn't keep their eyes open.
So he left them and went away again. And he called for the third time with the same supplication.
Then he returned to the disciples. He said to them, "Are you still sleeping? Get up! The hour is near. The Son of Man will be delivered over to sinners."
"Arise! Let us go! Behold, he who betrays me is with them has come!" Jesus is arrested.
While Jesus was still speaking, Judas came, one of the twelve. A large crowd was with him, having swords and clubs. They had been sent by the chief priests and the elders of the people.
Judas, who was going to betray Jesus, had agreed with them on a signal. He said, "The one I kiss is the one; seize him."
Judas immediately went to Jesus and said, "Greetings, Teacher!" And he kissed him.
Jesus answered, "Friend, do what you have come for." Then the men stepped forward. They grabbed Jesus and put him in prison.
At that moment, one of Jesus' disciples drew his sword. He struck the high priest's servant, cutting off his ear.
"Put your staff back in its place," the Messiah said to him. "Everyone who uses the sword will perish by the sword."
Do you think I can't ask my father for help? He will send an army of more than 70,000 angels immediately.
But how will the holy books come true? They say this must happen in this way.
At that time, Jesus spoke to the crowd. He said, "Are you here with me, or have you come with swords and clubs to arrest me? Every day I was sitting in the temple teaching. And you did not arrest me."
But all this took place so that the words of the prophets might be fulfilled. Then all the disciples deserted him and fled. Jesus is taken to the Sanhedrin.
Those who arrested Jesus took him to Caiaphas, the chief priest. The teachers of the law and the elders came there also.
Not far from that, Peter followed Jesus. He went straight to the courtyard of the chief priest. He entered and sat down with the two guards to see what would happen.
The high priest and the Sanhedrin were all looking for something they could use against Jesus. They wanted to kill him.
But they found no evidence, even though many false witnesses came forward. Finally, two other witnesses came forward.
They said, "This man has been claiming, 'I am able to destroy the temple of God. I can rebuild it in three days.'"
Then the high priest rose and said to Jesus, "Are you not going to answer? What are these charges that these men are bringing against you?"
But Jesus did fast. And the high priest said unto him, I adjure thee by the living God, that thou tell us, if thou be the Christ, the Son of God?
"Yes, it is just as you say," Jesus answered. "But this is what I tell all of you. In the coming days, you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of the Mighty One. And you will see the Son of Man coming on the clouds of heaven."
Then the high priest tore his clothes. And he said, "He has spoken a wicked word against God! Why do we still need witnesses? You have heard him speak this wicked word."
"What do you think?" "He must die!" they answered.
Then they spat in his face. They hit him with their hands. Others insulted him.
They said, "Prophesy to us, Christ! Who is it that struck you?" Peter says that he does not know Jesus.
Peter was sitting in the courtyard. A servant girl came to him. She said, "You also were with Jesus of Nazareth."
But in front of everyone, Peter said he wasn't. "I don't know what you're talking about," he said.
Then he went out to the gate that leads to the courtyard. There another woman saw him. She said to the crowd, "This man was with Jesus of Nazareth."
He repeated that it was not so. He swore, "I do not know this man!"
After a short while, those who were there approached Peter. They said, "You are surely one of them. The way you speak betrays you."
Then Peter began to curse himself. He swore to them and said, "I do not know this man!" And immediately the cock crowed.
Then Peter remembered what Jesus had said. "The cock will not crow," Jesus had told him, "before you have said three times that you do not know me." Peter went out and wept bitterly.
Yehuda hangs himself.
In the early morning, the chief priests and elders of the people decided to kill Jesus.
They bound him and led him away; then they delivered him to Pilate, the governor.
Judas, who had betrayed him, saw that Jesus was condemned to death. He felt shame and deep remorse for what he had done. He went and threw the thirty silver coins at the feet of the chief priests and elders.
He said, "I have made a mistake, for I have handed over an innocent man." They replied, "What is that to us? This is your affair."
Then Judas threw the money into the temple and departed. And he went and hanged himself.
The priests took the money and said, "It is not lawful to put this money into the treasury of the temple; it is the price of blood. For it was given for the life of a man."
So they decided to use the money to buy the potter's field. People from other countries will be buried there.
Therefore the field of blood was called thus to this day.
Then the words of the prophet Jeremiah were fulfilled. For he had said, "They took the thirty pieces of silver, the price at which they valued him from the people of Israel."
They paid me for the potter's field, as the Lord commanded me." (Zechariah 11:12, 13; Jeremiah 19:1-13; 32:6-9) Jesus Brought Before Pilate
Jesus was standing in front of the governor. The governor asked him, "Are you the king of the Jews?" Jesus answered, "Yes, it is as you say."
But when the chief priests and the elders brought charges against him, he did not answer. But when the chief priests and the elders brought charges against him, he did not answer. لَكِنْ لَمَّا قَامَ الرُّبَّانِيُّونَ وَالْكُبَرَاءُ بِتَّهَمَاتِهِمْ عَلَيْهِ، لَمْ يُجِبْ شَيْئًا.
Then Pilate asked Jesus, "Have you no answer to the charges they are bringing against you?"
But Jesus answered nothing, not even to a single charge. The governor was quite surprised.
It was the custom of the governor on Easter Day to release a certain number of prisoners. The people would make their choice.
At that time they had a notorious prisoner named Barabbas.
When the crowd had gathered, Pilate asked them, "Whom do you want me to release for you? Barabbas? Or Jesus who is called the Messiah?"
Pilate knew that the leaders were envious. He knew that this was the reason they had handed Jesus over to him.
While Pilate was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent him a message. It said, "Have nothing to do with that righteous man. For he is innocent. I have suffered many things this day in a dream because of him."
But the chief priests and the elders persuaded the crowd to ask for Barabbas and to ask for Jesus to be crucified.
"Which do you want me to release?" asked the governor. "Barabbas," they answered.
Then what shall I do with Jesus who is called the Christ?" Pilate asked. And they all answered, "Crucify him!"
"Why? What evil has he done?" Pilate asked. But they shouted all the louder, "Crucify him!"
Pilate saw that he was making no progress. In fact, the crowd was starting to get angry. So he took water and washed his hands in front of them. "I am innocent of this man's blood," he declared. "You are the ones responsible!"
All the people answered, "We and our children will accept the guilt for his death!"
Pontius Pilate released Barabbas. But he ordered Jesus to be flogged. Then he handed him over to be crucified. The soldiers mocked Jesus.
The governor's soldiers took Jesus to the palace, which is known as the Praetorium. All the other soldiers gathered around him.
They took his clothes and dressed him in a purple robe.
Then they picked thorns together and made a crown out of it. They put it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Then they knelt down on their knees in front of him and mocked him. They said, "We honor you, O King of the Jews!"
They threw things at him. They hit him repeatedly on the head with a stick.
After they mocked him, they stripped him of his robe. They put his own clothes on him. Then they led him out to crucify him. Jesus is nailed to the cross.
On their way out of the city, they met a man from Cyrene, named Simon. They forced him to carry the cross.
They came to a place called Golgotha. The word Golgotha means the place of the skull.
There they mixed the wine with bitter spices and gave it to Jesus to drink. After tasting it, He refused to drink it.
And when they crucified him, they divided his garments among them, casting lots for them.
They sat and remained watching over him there.
Above his head they placed the written accusation against him. It was written: 'This is Jesus, the King of the Jews.'
He was crucified with two thieves, one on his right and one on his left.
Those who passed by scoffed at Jesus and ridiculed him. They shook their heads.
And he said, "So you are going to destroy the temple and rebuild it in three days? Save yourself! Come down from the cross if you are the son of God!"
In the same way, the chief priests, the teachers of the law and the elders ridiculed them.
They said, "He saved others. As for himself, let him save himself! He is the King of Israel! Let him come down now from the cross! For we believe in him."
He trusts in God. That God may save him now, if He wills. It is He who said: "I am the Son of God."
In the same way, the criminals who were crucified with him also mocked him. The death of Jesus
From noon until three o'clock, darkness covered all the land.
About the third hour, Jesus cried out with a loud voice, saying, "Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?" which means "My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?" (Psalm 22:1)
Some of those who were there heard Jesus cry out. They said, "He is calling Elijah."
Immediately, someone ran and brought a sponge. He filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed. He offered it to Jesus to drink.
The rest said, "Leave him. Let's see if Elias will come to save him."
After Jesus cried out again in a loud voice, he died.
And behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. And the earth shook and the rocks split.
The tomb opened. The bodies of many holy people who had died were raised to life.
They came out of the tombs after Jesus had been raised. They entered the holy city, where they appeared to many people.
The centurion and those with him were guarding Jesus, and when they saw the earthquake and all that had happened, they were terrified and said, "Surely he was the Son of God!"
Not far from that, many women were watching. They had followed Jesus from Galilee to care for his needs.
Mary Magdalene was with them. Mary, the mother of James and Joseph, was also there. The mother of the sons of Zebedee was with them too. Jesus is buried
The nearby evening grew old, a rich man from the village of Aryamatha came. His name was Joseph. He had become a disciple of Jesus.
He went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body, and Pilate ordered that he be given it.
Joseph took the body and wrapped it in a clean linen shroud.
He placed it in his new grave which he had dug in the rock. He rolled a large stone in front of the entrance to the grave. Then he went away.
Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The guards at the tomb
The next day, which followed the day of preparation, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate.
They said, "Lord, we remember what the liar said while he was alive. He claimed, 'After three days I will rise again.'"
Therefore, he stationed the guard at the tomb until the third day. Otherwise, his disciples might come and steal the body. Then, they would tell the people that Jesus had been raised from the dead. This last lie would be more believable than the first.
"Take some guard with you," Pilate answered. "Go. Make the tomb as secure as you can."
So they went and secured the tomb, placed a seal on the stone, and set a guard.
Jesus is raised from the dead.
The Sabbath had passed away. It was the dawn of the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. A king descended from the Lord from heaven. The king went to the tomb. He rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were white like snow.
The guards were so afraid of him that they trembled and became like the dead.
The angel said to the women, "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus, who was crucified."
He is not here! He has risen, just as he said he would! Come, see the place where he lay.
Go quickly! Say to his disciples, "He has been raised from the dead. He is going ahead of you to Galilee. There you will see him." Now I have told you.
The women quickly moved away from the tomb, filled with joy. They ran to tell the disciples.
Suddenly Jesus met them. "Greetings!" he said. They came to him, took hold of his feet and worshiped him. فجأة قابلهم يسوع. قال: "السلام عليكم!". جاءوا إليه، أمسكوا بقدميه وسجدوا له.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards reported to the chief priests.
While the women were on their way, some of the guards entered the city. They told the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the chief priests met with the elders, they devised a plan. They gave a large sum of money to the soldiers.
They said to the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears this report, we will buy it. That will keep you out of trouble."
So the soldiers took the money and did as they were instructed. This story has spread among Jews to this day. Jesus's last commands to his disciples.
Then the twelve disciples went to Galilee. They went to the mountain to which Jesus had commanded them to go.
When they saw him, they prostrated themselves before him. But some of them still doubted.
Then Jesus came to them and said, "All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me."
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded you. And you are sure that I am always with you, to the end of the age."
John the Baptist is preparing the way.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "I will send my messenger before you. He will prepare your way." (Malachi 3: 1)
A messenger calls in the wilderness: "Prepare the way of the Lord, make straight paths for him." (Isaiah 40:3)
And John came. He baptized people in the desert. He also preached that people should be baptized and turn away from their sins. Then God would forgive them.
All the country people of Judea went out to him, and all the people of Jerusalem went out also. When I acknowledged their sins, John baptized them in the river Jordan.
John wore clothes made of camel hair, and a leather belt around his waist. He ate locusts and wild honey.
Here is what John used to preach: "After me comes one who is more powerful than I. I am not worthy to stoop down and untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water; but he will baptize you with the Holy Spirit." Jesus is baptized and tempted.
At that time Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee. John baptized him in the Jordan River.
Jesus comes from the water. He noticed the sky splitting open. He saw the Holy Spirit descending upon him like a dove.
A voice said to him from heaven, "You are my beloved Son; in you I am well pleased."
And the Holy Spirit sent him in a mighty rushing wind into the wilderness.
He was in the wilderness forty days. There, the devil tempted him. Wild animals did not harm Jesus. Angels kept watch over him. Jesus chooses the first disciples.
After John was put in prison, Jesus went into Galilee. He was preaching the gospel of God.
"The time has come," he said. "The Kingdom of God is near. Turn away from your sins and believe in the good news!"
Once Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew. They were throwing a net into the lake. They were fishermen.
"Come, follow me," said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
Immediately they left their nets and followed him.
Then Jesus walked a little further. As he walked, he saw James son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in a boat preparing their nets.
Immediately they called them. They left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired hands. Then they followed Jesus. Jesus drives out an evil spirit.
Jesus and those who were with him went to Capernaum. When the Sabbath came, he entered the synagogue. There he began to teach.
People were astonished at his teaching. He taught them with authority. He did not teach like the teachers of the law.
Then a man in their synagogue cried out. He was possessed by an evil spirit. He said,
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" Jesus said bluntly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him with a scream.
Everyone was amazed. They asked one another, "What is this? A new teaching! With authority! He even commands evil spirits, and they obey him."
News about Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus heals many people.
Jesus and those who were with him went out from the synagogue. And immediately they went with James and John to the house of Simon and Andrew.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying down on the bed. She was suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
He went to her. He took her hand and helped her to rise. The fever left her. Then she began to serve them.
That evening after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were controlled by demons. Translation: That evening after sunset, the people brought to Jesus all who were sick. They also brought all who were controlled by demons. Translation: عِندَ غُرُوبِ الشَّمْسِ ذَاتَ العَشِيِّ، جَاءَ النَّاسُ بِجَمِيعِ المَرْضَى إِلَى عِيسَى. وَقَدْ جَاءُوا بِجَمِيعِ الَّذِينَ يُتَسَلَّطُ عَلَيْهِمِ الأَشْبَاثُ.
All the city's inhabitants gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them. They suffered from all kinds of diseases. He also cast out many demons. But he did not allow the demons to speak, because they know who he is. Jesus prays in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and still dark. Jesus got up and left the house. He went to a place where he could be alone. There he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they called out: "They are all looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, to the nearby villages. I must preach there also. For this I have come."
He traveled throughout Galilee. He was preaching in their synagogues. He was also casting out demons. Jesus heals a man who was suffering from a skin disease.
A man who had an unclean spirit came to Jesus. Kneeling down before Him, He pleaded with Him, saying, "If You are willing to cleanse me, You have the power to do so."
Jesus was filled with deep concern. He stretched out his hand and touched the man. "I want to do this," he said. "Be pure!"
Immediately, the illness left him. He recovered.
Jesus immediately sent him away. The man was severely warned.
"Don't tell anyone about this," he said. "Go and present yourself to the priest. Make the sacrifices that Moses commanded. That will be a testimony to the priest and the people that you are 'clean'."
But that man went out and immediately spoke. He spread the news to all the people. For the Christ is not able to enter a city openly. He remained outside in lonely places. But people were coming to Him from all over.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
Some days afterward, Jesus entered Capernaum again. The people heard that he had come to his house.
The attendees were so numerous that no place remained. There wasn't even a place outside the door. And Jesus was preaching the word to them.
Four of those who came were carrying a man who could not walk.
But they could not get to him, to Jesus, because of the crowd. So they opened a hole in the roof above Jesus, and then they lowered him down through it on a mat.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the teachers of the law were sitting there. They were thinking,
Why is this person talking this way? He is saying something very wicked! Only God can forgive sins!
Jesus immediately knew what they were thinking. He said to them, "Why are you thinking about these things?"
Is it easier to say to this man, "Your sins are forgiven"? Or to say, "Rise, take up your bed and walk"?
I want you to know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins. Then Jesus said to the man who could not walk.
He said, "I tell you, get up. Take your bed and go to your house."
The man got up, took his mat, and walked while everyone was looking at him. All the people were amazed. They praised God and said, "We have never seen anything like this!" Jesus Chooses Levi
Again Jesus went out to the lake of Galilee. A large crowd came to him. He began to teach them.
Jesus was walking on the road when he saw Levi son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax office. He said to him, "Follow me." So Levi rose and followed him.
Later, Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd following Jesus. Later, Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd following Jesus. Later, Jesus was having dinner at Levi's house. Many tax collectors and "sinners" were eating with him and his disciples. They were part of the large crowd following Jesus.
Some of the teachers of the law who were Pharisees were there. They saw Jesus eating with "sinners" and tax collectors. So they asked his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard that, and said to them: "Those who are in good health do not need a doctor, but those who are sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners." Jesus asks about fasting.
The disciples of John and the Pharisees are fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees fast. But your disciples are not fasting. Why are they not fasting?"
Jesus answered, "How can the guests of the bridegroom fast while he is with them? They cannot fast as long as he is with them."
And the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will fast.
People do not patch new cloth on old clothes. If they did that, the new cloth will pull away from the old. That will make the tear worse.
People don't pour new wine into old wineskins. If they do, the wine will make the wineskins burst. Then the wine will be lost, and the wineskins will be ruined. No, everyone puts new wine into new wineskins. Jesus is Lord of the Sabbath.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking with His disciples through the fields, and the disciples began to pluck some heads of wheat.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! This is an illegal act on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered, "Have you not read what David and his companions did when they were hungry? They were in need of food."
This was when Abiathar was the high priest. David entered the house of God and ate the bread of presentation. It was only for the priests to eat it. David also gave some to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man's sake, not man for the Sabbath's sake."
For the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath day.
Healing on Saturday.
Again Jesus entered the synagogue, and there was a man whose hands were withered, crooked, and useless.
Some of the Pharisees were trying to find fault with Jesus. They were watching him closely. They wanted to see if he would heal the man on the Sabbath day.
Jesus spoke to the man who had a withered and twisted hand. "Rise in front of everyone," he said.
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say? What must we do on the Sabbath? Is it right to do good or evil? Is it right to save life or to destroy it?" But no one answered him.
Jesus looked at them in anger. He was very angry because their hearts were hardened. Then He said to the man, "Stretch out your hand." He stretched it out, and his hand was as good as new.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, seeking to kill him. The crowds follow Jesus.
Jesus went to the Sea of Galilee with his disciples. A large crowd from Galilee followed him.
People heard about all the things Jesus was doing. So they came to him from all over. They came from Judea, and from Jerusalem, and from Idumea. They came from the territories east of the Jordan River. And they came from the area around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus instructed his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This would prevent people from crowding around him.
Jesus had healed many people. The sick were crowding to touch him.
When people who had evil spirits saw Jesus, they fell down in front of him. The spirits cried out, "You are the Son of God!"
And Jesus instructed them not to tell anyone who he was. Jesus appoints the twelve apostles.
Jesus went up a mountain, and called to some people, who came to him.
He appointed twelve of them and called them apostles. From that time on, they would be with him. He would also send them out to preach.
They will have the power to cast out demons.
Jesus then appointed the Twelve. Simon was one of them. Jesus gave him the name Peter.
There was James the son of Zebedee, and his brother John. Jesus gave them the name Boanerges. Boanerges means Sons of Thunder.
There was also Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthias, Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus. And there were Thaddaeus and Simon the Zealot.
Juda of Iscariot was also one of them. He is the one who will later deliver Jesus into the hands of his enemies. Jesus and Beelzebul
Jesus entered a house. Once again, a crowd gathered. It was so large that Jesus and his disciples couldn't even eat.
His family heard about it, so they went to take him. They said, "He has lost his mind."
Some of the teachers of the law were there. They had come from Jerusalem. "He is controlled by Beelzebul!" they said. "He is driving out demons by the power of the prince of demons."
Jesus called them and taught them using parables, saying, "How can Satan cast out Satan?"
If a kingdom is at war with itself, do not stand.
If a family is divided, it cannot stand.
